### Instruction:
Implement a heap sort algorithm in Java without using any additional data structures or libraries. The algorithm should only use a fixed-size array to perform the sorting. Additionally, the array size should be limited to 100 elements and the implementation should be able to handle duplicate elements in the array efficiently. The algorithm should have a time complexity of O(n log n) and a space complexity of O(1).

### Response:
Here is a Java implementation of heap sort algorithm using a fixed-size array of maximum 100 elements. This implementation can handle duplicate elements efficiently.

```java
public class HeapSort {

    private static int heapSize;

    public static void sort(int[] array) {
        buildHeap(array);

        while (heapSize > 1) {
            swap(array, 0, heapSize - 1);
            heapSize--;
            heapify(array, 0);
        }
    }

    private static void buildHeap(int[] array) {
        heapSize = array.length;
        for (int i = (heapSize / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            heapify(array, i);
        }
    }

    private static void heapify(int[] array, int rootIndex) {
        int largestIndex = rootIndex;
        int leftChildIndex = 2 * rootIndex + 1;
        int rightChildIndex = 2 * rootIndex + 2;

        if (leftChildIndex < heapSize && array[leftChildIndex] > array[largestIndex]) {
            largestIndex = leftChildIndex;
        }

        if (rightChildIndex < heapSize && array[rightChildIndex] > array[largestIndex]) {
            largestIndex = rightChildIndex;
        }

        if (largestIndex != rootIndex) {
            swap(array, rootIndex, largestIndex);
            heapify(array, largestIndex);
        }
    }

    private static void swap(int[] array, int index1, int index2) {
        int temp = array[index1];
        array[index1] = array[index2];
        array[index2] = temp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {9, 4, 8, 3, 1, 9, 6, 2, 4, 7};
        sort(array);

        System.out.println("Sorted array:");
        for (int num : array) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation builds a max heap from the given array using the `buildHeap` method. Then, it repeatedly swaps the root element (maximum element) with the last element of the heap, reduces the heap size, and heapifies the new root to maintain the max heap property. Finally, it prints the sorted array.

The time complexity of heap sort is O(n log n) and the space complexity is O(1) since the sorting is done in place without using any additional data structures.

### Instruction:
Please write a function in Java to sort a given array of integers into ascending order using a merge sort algorithm. The function should have the following signature:
void mergeSort(int[] array, int low, int high)

The function should sort the elements in the array from index low to index high (inclusive) using the merge sort algorithm. The merge sort algorithm works as follows:

1. Divide the array into two halves, called left and right.
2. Recursively sort the left half of the array.
3. Recursively sort the right half of the array.
4. Merge the sorted left and right halves into a single sorted array.

Note: The merge step should be implemented using an auxiliary array to minimize memory usage. The merge operation should be performed in-place, meaning that the original array should be modified directly without creating a new array.

Example:
int[] array = {9, 5, 2, 7, 1, 8};
mergeSort(array, 0, array.length - 1);
// The array should be sorted in ascending order: {1, 2, 5, 7, 8, 9}

Please write the code for the mergeSort function.

### Response:
```
public class MergeSort {

  public static void mergeSort(int[] array, int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
      int mid = (low + high) / 2;
      mergeSort(array, low, mid);
      mergeSort(array, mid + 1, high);
      merge(array, low, mid, high);
    }
  }

  private static void merge(int[] array, int low, int mid, int high) {
    int[] temp = new int[high - low + 1];
    int i = low;
    int j = mid + 1;
    int k = 0;

    while (i <= mid && j <= high) {
      if (array[i] <= array[j]) {
        temp[k++] = array[i++];
      } else {
        temp[k++] = array[j++];
      }
    }

    while (i <= mid) {
      temp[k++] = array[i++];
    }

    while (j <= high) {
      temp[k++] = array[j++];
    }

    for (int x = low; x <= high; x++) {
      array[x] = temp[x - low];
    }
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] array = {9, 5, 2, 7, 1, 8};
    mergeSort(array, 0, array.length - 1);
    for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
      System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
    }
  }
}
```

