The program should take an input array of integers and two target values: lower and upper. It should then return the indices of the first and last occurrences of any value within the range [lower, upper] in the array. If no such value exists, the program should return -1 for both indices.

To solve this problem, you should first implement a binary search algorithm to find the first occurrence of the lower value in the array. Once you find this occurrence, you should iterate backwards from this index to find the first occurrence of any value that is less than the lower value.

Next, you should implement another binary search algorithm to find the last occurrence of the upper value in the array. Once you find this occurrence, you should iterate forwards from this index to find the last occurrence of any value that is greater than the upper value.

Finally, your program should return the indices of the first and last occurrences found. If no occurrences are found, it should return -1 for both indices.

Make sure to handle edge cases such as empty arrays or arrays where the lower value is greater than the upper value. Also, consider optimizing your implementation to have a time complexity of O(log n) for each binary search operation.

In addition to the previous requirements, your program should also handle the case where the input array may contain duplicates. In this case, your program should return the indices of the first and last occurrences of the unique values within the range [lower, upper]. If there are multiple occurrences of a unique value within the range, your program should return the indices of the first and last occurrences among the duplicates.

To achieve this, you will need to modify your binary search algorithm to handle duplicates. Instead of returning the index of the first occurrence of the target value, your binary search should return the index of the first occurrence of the unique value among duplicates.

You should also modify the iteration process to find the first occurrence of a value that is less than the lower value, and the last occurrence of a value that is greater than the upper value, to consider duplicates as well.

