Replace the previous requirement with a less common one: Write a C program that uses a function pointer to implement a binary search tree data structure. The program should allow the user to insert, search, and delete nodes in the binary search tree. Additionally, explain the concept of a self-balancing binary search tree and discuss its advantages over a regular binary search tree.

In addition to the previous requirements, please also explain the concept of callback functions and provide an example of how to use a function pointer as a callback function in the binary search tree program.

### Response:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Binary Search Tree Node
typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* left;
    struct Node* right;
} Node;

// Function pointer type alias
typedef void (*TreeFunction)(Node**, int);

// Function to insert a node into the binary search tree
void insert(Node** root, int data) {
    if (*root == NULL) {
        Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
        newNode->data = data;
        newNode->left = NULL;
        newNode->right = NULL;
        *root = newNode;
    } else if (data < (*root)->data) {
        insert(&(*root)->left, data);
    } else {
        insert(&(*root)->right, data);
    }
}

// Function to search for a node in the binary search tree
void search(Node* root, int data) {
    if (root == NULL) {
        printf("Node not found.\n");
    } else if (data < root->data) {
        search(root->left, data);
    } else if (data > root->data) {
        search(root->right, data);
    } else {
        printf("Node found.\n");
    }
}

// Function to delete a node from the binary search tree
void delete(Node** root, int data) {
    if (*root == NULL) {
        printf("Node not found.\n");
    } else if (data < (*root)->data) {
        delete(&(*root)->left, data);
    } else if (data > (*root)->data) {
        delete(&(*root)->right, data);
    } else {
        // Node found, perform deletion
        if ((*root)->left == NULL && (*root)->right == NULL) {
            free(*root);
            *root = NULL;
        } else if ((*root)->left == NULL) {
            Node* temp = *root;
            *root = (*root)->right;
            free(temp);
        } else if ((*root)->right == NULL) {
            Node* temp = *root;
            *root = (*root)->left;
            free(temp);
        } else {
            Node* successor = (*root)->right;
            while (successor->left != NULL) {
                successor = successor->left;
            }
            (*root)->data = successor->data;
            delete(&(*root)->right, successor->data);
        }
    }
}

// Function to perform in-order traversal of the binary search tree
void inOrderTraversal(Node* root) {
    if (root != NULL) {
        inOrderTraversal(root->left);
        printf("%d ", root->data);
        inOrderTraversal(root->right);
    }
}

int main() {
    Node* root = NULL;

    // Insert nodes into the binary search tree
    insert(&root, 50);
    insert(&root, 30);
    insert(&root, 20);
    insert(&root, 40);
    insert(&root, 70);
    insert(&root, 60);
    insert(&root, 80);

    // Print the binary search tree using in-order traversal
    printf("In-order traversal of the binary search tree: ");
    inOrderTraversal(root);
    printf("\n");

    // Search for a node in the binary search tree
    search(root, 40);

    // Delete a node from the binary search tree
    delete(&root, 30);

    // Print the binary search tree after deletion using in-order traversal
    printf("In-order traversal of the binary search tree after deletion: ");
    inOrderTraversal(root);
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}

A self-balancing binary search tree, such as an AVL tree or a Red-Black tree, is a binary search tree that automatically keeps its height balanced. This means that the heights of the left and right subtrees of every node differ by at most one, ensuring efficient search, insert, and delete operations with a worst-case time complexity of O(log n). In a regular binary search tree, if the elements are inserted in sorted order, the tree becomes linear, leading to a worst-case time complexity of O(n) for search, insert, and delete operations.

A callback function, also known as a function pointer, is a function that is passed as an argument to another function and is called within that function. The callback function can be used to customize the behavior of the function that receives it. In the binary search tree program, we can use a function pointer as a callback function to perform a custom action during a specific operation, such as printing the value of each node during an in-order traversal.

Example of using a function pointer as a callback function in the binary search tree program:

