Write a Java method to find the index of a given string in an array of strings. Additionally, if the string is not found in the array, return -1. You are not allowed to use any built-in functions or libraries that directly solve the problem. Implement the method in a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the size of the array. Furthermore, you are now required to handle the scenario where the array may contain duplicates of the given string and return the index of the first occurrence of the string in the array.

### Response:
Here's an example of how you can implement the method to find the index of a given string in an array of strings, while also handling duplicates:

```java
public class StringArraySearch {

  public static int findStringIndex(String[] arr, String target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
      int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

      if (arr[mid].equals(target)) {
        // Check if this is the first occurrence of the string
        if (mid == 0 || !arr[mid - 1].equals(target)) {
          return mid;
        } else {
          // Move the right pointer to search for the first occurrence
          right = mid - 1;
        }
      } else if (arr[mid].compareTo(target) < 0) {
        left = mid + 1;
      } else {
        right = mid - 1;
      }
    }

    return -1;
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    String[] arr = {"apple", "banana", "banana", "grape", "orange"};
    String target = "banana";
    int index = findStringIndex(arr, target);

    if (index == -1) {
      System.out.println("String not found in the array.");
    } else {
      System.out.println("Index of first occurrence: " + index);
    }
  }
}
```

In this implementation, we use a binary search algorithm to search for the target string in the array. We keep track of the left and right indices to divide the search space in each iteration. If the middle element is equal to the target string, we check if it is the first occurrence. If it is, we return the index. Otherwise, we move the right pointer to search for the first occurrence. If the middle element is smaller than the target, we update the left index. If it is larger, we update the right index. The search continues until the left and right indices cross each other, indicating that the target string was not found.

