Find the lowest common ancestor in a binary search tree in C++. Implement a function that takes in the root of the tree and two nodes, and returns the lowest common ancestor of the two nodes. The function should handle the following cases:

1. If one of the nodes is the root itself, return the root.
2. If the two nodes are the same, return the node itself.
3. If one of the nodes is an ancestor of the other node, return the ancestor node.
4. If both nodes are in the left subtree of the root, recursively call the function on the left subtree.
5. If both nodes are in the right subtree of the root, recursively call the function on the right subtree.
6. If one node is in the left subtree and the other is in the right subtree, return the root.
7. If both nodes are not present in the tree, return NULL.
8. If the binary search tree is not balanced, optimize the algorithm to have a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the number of nodes in the tree.

### Response:
Here's an implementation of the function in C++:

```cpp
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* left;
    Node* right;
};

Node* findLowestCommonAncestor(Node* root, Node* node1, Node* node2) {
    if (root == NULL || node1 == NULL || node2 == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    if (root == node1 || root == node2) {
        return root;
    }

    if (node1->data < root->data && node2->data < root->data) {
        return findLowestCommonAncestor(root->left, node1, node2);
    }

    if (node1->data > root->data && node2->data > root->data) {
        return findLowestCommonAncestor(root->right, node1, node2);
    }

    return root;
}
```

To optimize the algorithm for a balanced binary search tree, we can take advantage of the property that the left subtree contains values less than the root, and the right subtree contains values greater than the root. By comparing the values of the nodes with the root, we can determine which subtree to search in.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n) in a balanced binary search tree, where n is the number of nodes in the tree. This is because in each recursive call, the number of remaining nodes to search is halved.

