The provided code is almost correct, but there is a minor error in the base case condition of the `findMaxAndMin` function. The condition `else if (low > high)` should be changed to `else if (low >= high)`. This change is necessary to handle the case when the array has only one element (low == high), as in that case, both the maximum and minimum values would be the same.

Here is the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void findMaxAndMin(int arr[], int low, int high, int& maxVal, int& minVal) {
    if (low == high) {
        maxVal = minVal = arr[low];
        return;
    } else if (low >= high) {
        return;
    }

    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    int leftMax, leftMin;
    findMaxAndMin(arr, low, mid, leftMax, leftMin);
    int rightMax, rightMin;
    findMaxAndMin(arr, mid + 1, high, rightMax, rightMin);

    maxVal = max(leftMax, rightMax);
    minVal = min(leftMin, rightMin);
}

int main() {
