```javascript
function findMaxNumber(arr) {
  if (arr.length === 1) {
    return arr[0];
  }

  const midIndex = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const leftArr = arr.slice(0, midIndex);
  const rightArr = arr.slice(midIndex);

  const leftMax = findMaxNumber(leftArr);
  const rightMax = findMaxNumber(rightArr);

  return Math.max(leftMax, rightMax);
}
```

Explanation:
- The function takes an array `arr` as input.
- If the length of the array is 1, we've reached the base case and we simply return the single element.
- Otherwise, we find the middle index of the array using `Math.floor(arr.length / 2)`.
- We split the array into two halves, `leftArr` and `rightArr`, using `slice()` method.
- We recursively call `findMaxNumber()` on both halves to find the maximum number in each half.
- Finally, we compare the maximum numbers of the two halves using `Math.max()` and return the maximum value.

The time complexity of this function is O(n log n) because at each level of recursion, we split the array in half, resulting in log n levels, and at each level, we perform a constant-time operation of finding the maximum using `Math.max()`. The overall space complexity is O(log n) because at each level of recursion, we only store the two halves of the array.

