Additionally, the program should handle duplicate values in the array by returning the index of the first occurrence of the target integer. If there are multiple occurrences of the target integer, the program should return the index of the leftmost occurrence.

In addition to the above requirements, the program should also be able to handle arrays with negative integers. The program should correctly handle the search for negative target integers in the array and return the correct index. The program should handle cases where both the array and the target integer are negative, as well as cases where only one of them is negative.

The program should also handle arrays that contain floating-point numbers. The binary search algorithm should be modified to handle searching for a target floating-point number in the array. The program should return the index of the closest number to the target floating-point number if the exact target number is not found in the array. If there are multiple closest numbers, the program should return the index of the leftmost closest number.

Furthermore, the program should be able to handle large input arrays with billions of elements. The program should efficiently perform the binary search on such large arrays without causing memory or performance issues. The program should also handle extreme cases where the target integer is located at the beginning or end of the array, as well as cases where the array is completely filled with duplicate target integers.

Finally, the program should implement error handling to gracefully handle any exceptions or errors that may occur during the execution of the binary search algorithm. The program should provide informative error messages and continue running without crashing or terminating abruptly.

### Response:
Here is an example implementation of a Java program that meets the requirements described:

```java
public class BinarySearch {
    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                // Check for leftmost occurrence
                while (mid > 0 && arr[mid - 1] == target) {
                    mid--;
                }
                return mid;
            }

            if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }

    public static int binarySearch(double[] arr, double target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;
        int closestIndex = -1;
        double minDiff = Double.MAX_VALUE;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            double diff = Math.abs(arr[mid] - target);

            if (diff < minDiff) {
                minDiff = diff;
                closestIndex = mid;
            }

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                // Check for leftmost occurrence
                while (mid > 0 && arr[mid - 1] == target) {
                    mid--;
                }
                return mid;
            }

            if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return closestIndex;
    }

